  "meta": {
    "title": "Slick and Seam: Alcanivorax Rides the Oil-Snow Column",
    "date": "2026-11-16",
    "lesson": "When oil spills into the open ocean it doesn't just sit on the surface — weathered droplets aggregate with mucus and dead plankton into 'marine oil snow' that sinks, carrying hydrocarbon-degrading specialists like Alcanivorax down through the water column. These obligate oil-eaters carry alkane hydroxylase to crack the lipid chains others can't touch, giving them a near-monopoly on a sudden lipid-rich food source — but that advantage evaporates as the slick disperses and ordinary protein/carbohydrate feeders retake the column. This level asks you to ride that narrow window: dominate the lipid class while it lasts, sense your way down the thermocline toward where nutrient-rich deep water is mixing up into the slick's base, and survive the grazers and phage that build up around any sudden bloom.",
    "citation": "Deepwater Horizon spill marine-oil-snow sedimentation (MOSSFA) studies, Gulf of Mexico, 2010-2015.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Deepwater Horizon oil spill, Gulf of Mexico - marine oil snow sedimentation and Alcanivorax/Oleispira bloom dynamics"
  },
